+1.4 Put $m=2d$ and $\delta=Z-z$. The admitted fixed-point equation gives $\|\delta\|\le |h|\|F(Z)\|/2\le |h|M/2$, hence $\delta=O(|h|)$ uniformly. Since $H\in C^3$, $F\in C^2$. Apply the scalar theorem [F4] to each $F_i$ along $[z,Z]$: $F_i(z+\delta)=F_i(z)+DF_i(z)\delta+R_i$, where $R_i=\tfrac12D^2F_i(z+\vartheta_i\delta)[\delta,\delta]$ for some $\vartheta_i\in(0,1)$. These points may differ with $i$. Bounded second derivatives on the tube give $|R_i|\le C_i\|\delta\|^2$, hence $\|R\|_2\le(\sum_{i=1}^m C_i^2)^{1/2}\|\delta\|^2$. Substitution into $\delta=(h/2)F(z+\delta)$ first gives $\delta=(h/2)F(z)+O(h^2)$ and then $\delta=(h/2)F(z)+(h^2/4)DF(z)F(z)+O(|h|^3)$. Thus $2Z-z=z+hF(z)+(h^2/2)DF(z)F(z)+O(|h|^3)$. For the exact flow, write $g_i(t)=(\varphi_t(z))_i$. By [F3] and [F6], $g_i^{(1)}=F_i(\varphi_t(z))$, $g_i^{(2)}=DF_i(\varphi_t(z))F(\varphi_t(z))$, and $g_i^{(3)}=D^2F_i(\varphi_t(z))[F(\varphi_t(z)),F(\varphi_t(z))]+DF_i(\varphi_t(z))DF(\varphi_t(z))F(\varphi_t(z))$ are defined, with the third derivative uniformly bounded on the controlled trajectories. Scalar Taylor in time gives $g_i(h)=z_i+hF_i(z)+(h^2/2)(DF(z)F(z))_i+(h^3/6)g_i^{(3)}(\eta_i h)$ for $h\ne0$, with possibly distinct $\eta_i\in(0,1)$. Each last term is bounded by $C_i'|h|^3$; summing their squares gives a Euclidean remainder bound $(\sum_{i=1}^m(C_i')^2)^{1/2}|h|^3$. The case $h=0$ is immediate. The exact flow therefore has the same terms through degree two as $2Z-z$, giving the uniform one-step defect $C|h|^3$ for either sign of $h$. No common Lagrange intermediate point for the vector is used. [A1, F3, F4, F6, given, algebra]
 
